  "strategy": "## 1. Approach and Rationale\n\nThis approach uses coordinate-ascent optimization with structured trial functions built from Fourier series and basis splines, optimized using quasi-Newton methods. We leverage the following key insights:\n\n- The optimal solution has a convoluted structure with many small peaks to avoid self-overlap\n- The functional form can be parameterized by a few smooth curves rather than turbulent grid values\n- The optimization landscape is discontinuous but separable through spectral projection\n\nThe central idea is to represent the step function through four orthogonal components:\n1. Frequencies from Fourier basis\n2. B-splines for higher-fidelity shape fitting\n3. Adaptive thresholding to enforce [0,1] bounds\n4. Stochastic rounding to encourage step-function character\n\nThis structured search addresses the limitations of grid-based methods and crude parameterization:\n\n1. Efficiency gains - optimized over a compressed manifold rather than full grid\n2. Structure-preserving - maintains functional forms likely to generalize well\n3. Intuition maintenance - keeps inversion principle of the Erd\u0151s problem\n\n### Bottlenecks Addressed\n- Grid orientation: replaces finite grids with continuous projection\n- Dimensionality: reduces 45+ dimensions to ~15 curve aligning parameters\n- Smoothness conflict: incorporates discontinuities through tailored StepFunction approach\n\n### Limitations/Hypotheses\n1. Hypothesis: Euler (log-sum-exp) loss will smooth non-optimal overlaps  \n2. Justification: Matches penalty structure without arbitrary cutoffs  \n3. Working assumption: B-spline curvatures will align with optimal self-avoiding overlaps",
